I. CURRENT CONTEXT AND EFPIA'S GENERAL COMMENTS:

EFPIA members would like first and foremost to re-anchor the remind that illicit, substandard and falsified products have on patient safety and, overall, society. They seriously harm or kill patients, challenge healthcare systems and undermine people's trust in quality medicines. In the context of a global pandemic and vaccination campaign, we believe that this point should be high on the EU legislator's mind when drafting this Regulation. We have learned, even more in the current period, that trust is the key of a successful health strategy. To give a worldwide context to the magnitude of the issue, according to the OECD/EUIPO (2019) study, the value of global trade in counterfeit pharmaceuticals was up to USD 4.4 billion in 2016 and direct and indirect job losses are estimated at more than 80 000 jobs in the EU1. EFPIA, therefore, appreciates the Commission's great progress and achievement in this area especially regarding the proposal for a Regulation on a Single Market for Digital Services (Digital Services Act or the DSA). This is undoubtedly a first step in the right direction. Directive 2000/31/EC has proven to be evidently not sufficient to address all the issues linked to the online space and in particular to effectively protect consumer's safety. Moreover, the development of digital technology along with the spread of online illegal activities noticeably requires a new legal regime to adapt with the current context. EFPIA fully understands and welcomes the philosophy of this text which is to:

- Better protect consumers and their fundamental rights online;
- Establish a powerful transparency and a clear accountability framework for online platforms;
- Foster innovation, growth and competitiveness within the single market. Naturally, the right balance between these principles has to be found. But when it comes to the protection of consumers, the EU legislator has an obligation to protect EU citizens from the potential harm caused by the online purchase of illicit, substandard or falsified pharmaceutical products. It will really be a missed opportunity to let consumers continue to be at risk if we do not seize this opportunity to update more profoundly the 2000 e-commerce Directive.

It will really be a missed opportunity to let consumers continue to be at risk if we do not seize this opportunity to update more profoundly the 2000 e-commerce Directive. Having reviewed the proposal for the DSA, we are nevertheless satisfied to see a number of welcome and interesting provisions, such as:

- Voluntary own-initiative investigations and legal compliance (Article 6);
- Obligation for legal representatives for all the online intermediaries (Article 10);
- Transparency reporting obligations for providers of intermediary services (Article 13);
- Notification of suspicions of criminal offences (Article 21);
- The publication of trader's information (Article 22) - even if we believe that in order to be efficient, this verification of the trader's information should not only be obtained but also be verified by the online platform and this provision should also apply to all intermediaries, and particularly, to domain names registrars;

