- Compliance officers (Article 32);
- The "European Board for Digital Services" and "Digital Services Coordinator of establishment" (Article 47). That being said, EFPIA is of the opinion that there is room for improvement of the proposed act. Especially with regards to the protection of the patient who easily has access to any kind of illegal content related to pharmaceutical products putting his or her health at high risk. There are various types of illegal content, but specific attention and therefore specific measures should be considered for illegal content related to dangerous products, namely "dangerous illegal content". This point was mentioned very recently during the 25th European IP Forum organized by UNIFAB2. At this occasion, Mr. Cédric O, French Secretary of State emphasized the particularities of dangerous contents and dangerous goods which "require a specific delimitation and response related to their dangerousness or the specific problems they raise" and thus, Mr. Cédric O believed that online platforms should have higher standards and obligations in the fight against these harmful products3. Similarly, Mr. Olivier DUSSOPT, Deputy Minister in charge of publ ic Accounts, pointed to the seriousness of counterfeited medical products during the sanitary crisis, which " compromises the health and safety of consumers". Mr. DUSSOPT emphasized that "we need to follow through on proceedings where the health and safety of our citizens is at stake".4

Indeed, illicit, substandard or falsified medicines/medical devices put patients' and consumers' health and safety in danger. It also generates environmental high risks.

